WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:02.440
Let’s face it.

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:08.050
Between English classes, Lit classes, World
Culture classes, and History classes, there’s

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:11.740
no escaping The Epic Poem.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:18.240
THE ODYSSEY, THE ILIAD, THE AENEID, THE EPIC
OF GILGAMESH, BEOWULF.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:24.449
At some point, we’re going to have to confront
the lyrical beauty of big strong men in big

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:30.650
strong armor fighting big strong monsters,
saving their kingdoms, and loving on their

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:31.650
ladies.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:40.320
Or loving their male best friend —but that’s
another poem.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.380
Today we’re going to take a look at one
particular Epic Poem and follow its long,

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:51.770
winding journey from way, way, way long ago
allllll the way to the present day, and we’ll

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:56.240
interrogate its relevance to our lives in
the here and now.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:03.760
Come now, Geats and Danes, to the violent,
bloody, mythic, mysterious world of BEOWULF.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:04.760
[INTRO]

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:09.119
To see BEOWULF’s malleability in action,
look no further than the very first word of

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:10.119
the poem.

00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:15.100
BEOWULF has a sort of “once upon a time”
structure, wherein the narrator is telling

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.850
us a tale.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:29.649
He has to get our attention somehow, so he
starts off by exclaiming, “Hwæt!”

00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:36.829
A lot of older versions use stuffier translations
like “Lo!” or “Hark!” but you gotta

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:42.310
party like it’s 1999, so Seamus Heaney begins
his translation with “So.”

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:48.679
It’s Friday afternoon and your Lit teacher
assigns you a five-page book report due Monday

00:01:48.679 --> 00:01:54.399
on Jimmy Squibbles’s most recent magnum
opus, HYPE HOUSE BLOOD BATH.

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:57.240
And you start with the basics.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.999
HYPE HOUSE BLOOD BATH by…

00:01:58.999 --> 00:02:00.159
Jimmy Squibbles.

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:02.859
Author, okay, check.

00:02:02.859 --> 00:02:09.020
Some historical context now: Written at Jimmy’s
Salton Sea lakehouse in the summer of 2018.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.020
Check.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.890
Hey, this book report stuff ain’t so bad
after all!

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:20.640
Okay, hotshot, now try that with BEOWULF.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.320
BEOWULF… written in… uh…

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.380
When was BEOWULF written anyway?

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.110
Okay, let’s try a softball.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:35.190
Who wrote BEOWULF?

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:47.730
Wait a minute… was BEOWULF even written
at all?

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:51.640
While scholars agree on all things HYPE HOUSE
BLOOD BATH—and how could they not, it’s

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.590
such a tightly-written piece of work—they
really don’t agree on much when it comes

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.190
to BEOWULF.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:01.380
Except one thing—the plot!

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.990
Pagan Scandinavia, 6th century.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.230
King Hrothgar’s mead hall.

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:11.460
Mead is an alcohol made with fermented honey,
and a mead hall is a big building with one

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:16.010
big room where… you guessed it—much mead
is enjoyed.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:18.720
Rowdy places, those mead halls.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:25.820
Hrothgar is King of the Danes, a North Germanic
tribe in southern Scandinavia, an area that

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:33.750
today includes Denmark proper and Skåneland,
which is the southern tip of the Scandinavian

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:35.930
Peninsula.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:41.370
One night, a monster named Grendel comes to
crash Hrothgar’s mead hall festivities and

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:47.310
ends up killing most of Hrothgar’s men.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:52.060
Enter Beowulf, a mighty warrior charged with
killing Grendel.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:57.930
Beowulf is a Geat, another North Germanic
tribe that resides in the Land of the Geats.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:05.000
And Beowulf does what anyone would do before
they fight a fearsome monster—he gets into

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.900
his birthday suit.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:19.850
The way Beowulf sees it, if Grendel doesn’t
have weapons or armor, why should he?

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:25.540
Beowulf slays Grendel, and there is much celebration
and even more mead.

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:29.990
But Grendel’s mother is having none of this.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:35.400
Beowulf killed her boy, y’all.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:43.320
So she attacks Hrothgar’s mead hall, Beowulf
kills her, and there is much celebration.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:46.570
And much mead.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:51.970
So much mead.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:57.820
Beowulf bids Hrothgar and his Danes farewell,
returns home to Geatland, and becomes King

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:59.600
of the Geats.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:00.680
Fifty years go by.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.060
Then one day, boom, a dragon attacks!

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:09.910
By this time Beowulf is old and isn’t as
spry as he once was.

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:10.980
Plus it’s a dragon.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:16.270
I feel like this is a job for that Hiccup
kid he trains dragons.

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:21.280
He defeats the dragon—I mean, this is Beowulf
we’re talking about—but not before the

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.420
dragon mortally h-wounds him.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:31.850
Beowulf’s body is cremated, a tower is built
in his honor, and I assume much mead is also

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:32.850
had.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:37.240
Beowulf, the mighty warrior, will live on
in our memories, and his legend will be told

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.440
for generations.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:43.860
Now… how that story is told is up for much
debate.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:50.230
Here’s the opening line of Seamus Heaney’s
introduction to his 1999 translation of BEOWULF:

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:56.130
“The poem called BEOWULF was composed sometime
between the middle of the seventh and the

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:02.430
end of the tenth century of the first millennium,
in the language that is to-day called Anglo-Saxon

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.150
or Old English.”

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:10.410
Right off the bat, we’re dealing with a
window of 300+ years as to when BEOWULF could

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:14.680
have been composed - that’s like comparing
today to 1821.

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:19.780
We were only five US presidents deep by then.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:25.210
And to further complicate matters, we don’t
even know what form that composition took.

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:30.470
It very well could have begun as a story passed
down through oral tradition, perpetually evolving

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:34.419
and changing only to then be later written
down.

00:06:34.419 --> 00:06:40.000
This oral tradition theory opens up a whole
can of worms for scholars and is—you guessed

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.050
it!—”up for much debate”!

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:47.910
See, if BEOWULF began as a story told around
the campfire, it would be nearly impossible

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:53.900
to trace it back to a so-called “author”
because it probably wouldn’t even have one.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:58.310
Detractors of the oral tradition theory cite
a number of issues, not the least of which

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:01.320
is the sheer complexity of BEOWULF.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:06.979
To them, the sophisticated language, structure,
and themes are so complex that had they somehow

00:07:06.979 --> 00:07:12.360
been verbally passed down, they would have
had a nearly impossible chance of surviving

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.600
the messy game of telephone that is oral tradition.

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:21.570
Regardless of how BEOWULF came to be written
down, we do have a manuscript.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:26.220
Says translator Seamus Heaney: “We know
about the poem more or less by chance because

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:29.370
it exists in one manuscript only.”

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:35.420
And boy oh boy is she a scrappy survivor,
barely making it through a fire in the eighteenth

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:36.420
century.

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:43.910
Our dating for the manuscript is between 975
and 1025, a window of only fifty years, which,

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:46.440
for BEOWULF is like a dang bullseye.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:51.590
And whatever mysteries already surrounded
BEOWULF, they ballooned exponentially when

00:07:51.590 --> 00:08:00.389
the text was “then transcribed and titled,
retranscribed and edited, translated and adapted,

00:08:00.389 --> 00:08:05.250
interpreted and reinterpreted, until it has
become canonical.”

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:13.440
And even though BEOWULF is fantastical as
all get-out, it is deeply interwoven with

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:20.729
real-life elements that not only muddy the
waters as to what aspects of BEOWULF are historical,

00:08:20.729 --> 00:08:25.300
but also give levity to some of the more outrageous
stuff.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:27.750
These historical figures,…

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:29.100
...clans,...

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.800
...and events...

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:36.890
...certainly make it tough to untangle the
historical from the fantastical.

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:43.060
All this talk of monsters and dragons and
the use of historical elements as a way to

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:48.200
lend believability to fantasy… guess it’s
only a matter of time before we talk about

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:52.230
the man himself: J.R.R Tolkien.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:55.360
BEOWULF was a huge influence on Tolkien and
his work.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:00.610
So much so that he’s widely regarded as
the man responsible for kickstarting modern

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:02.220
criticism of BEOWULF.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:07.980
Tolkien’s argument was that scholars had
been so wrapped up in the historicity of BEOWULF

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:11.250
that they were neglecting its value as a piece
of literature.

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:18.060
In his 1936 lecture “BEOWULF: The Monsters
and the Critics," Tolkien noted that BEOWULF

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:25.170
“is in fact so interesting as poetry, in
places poetry so powerful, that this quite

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.860
overshadows the historical content…”

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:33.640
Indeed, the allure of BEOWULF is so powerful
that it can transform a bored student into

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:35.550
a bonafide translator.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:40.460
Maria Dahvana Headley, whose vibrant 2020
translation of BEOWULF views the poem and

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:46.290
its characters through a modern lens, admits
that even she didn’t really take to BEOWULF

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:47.290
at first.

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:51.610
After reading an old translation, pre-Seamus
Heaney’s 1999 version, she reflected,

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:54.780
“I’m not going to lie to you.

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:59.730
I read it, and I thought, “All of the elements
are here for this to be thrilling.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:02.230
And instead it feels just kind of flat.”

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:08.360
Headley’s experience with BEOWULF is a prime
example of the now self-perpetuating legacy

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:09.510
of the poem.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:14.570
It’s been studied and translated so often
that younger generations continue to discover

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:18.120
different ways of connecting with the material.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:23.490
While the poem has been translated into thirty-eight
other languages, the number of translations

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:25.740
and adaptations varies.

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:30.010
True BEOWULF style, we can’t even agree
on the number of translations.

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:36.490
In British academic Andy Orchard’s 2003
A Critical Companion to Beowulf, he cites

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:40.960
thirty-three "representative" translations
in his bibliography.

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:46.010
Also in 2003 An annotated list from the Arizona
Center for Medieval and Renaissance Studies

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:51.120
that cites more than 300 translations and
adaptations.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:57.600
The BEOWULF's Afterlives Bibliographic Database
online lists an ever-growing number of translations,

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.430
and adaptations.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.970
As of the time of this recording, the number’s
at 743—

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:06.910
[Princess touches her earpiece] Wait, what’s
that?

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:08.040
Ah, 744.

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:09.040
Great.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:16.520
BEOWULF is so popular I’m -- 745- going
to have to remake this video every month,

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:18.100
aren’t I?

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:22.910
BEOWULF is the gift that keeps on giving,
continually evolving as each of us brings

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:25.420
our own experiences to it.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:28.160
We change the text and the text changes us.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:33.140
BEOWULF scholar Chris Jones likens BEOWULF
to other poems, which “move through time,

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:39.350
being kept alive by readings, misreadings,
re-imaginings, and accruing stories and significance

00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:41.690
around themselves as they go.

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:44.320
They are stories, as well as telling stories.”

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:49.180
BEOWULF, he says, “is not an object fixed
in the past.”

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:55.320
Even if we never ever EVER find out anything
more about BEOWULF—who wrote it, where it

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:59.500
came from, when it came from—we still have
BEOWULF.

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:04.720
So of course, historical context matters,
cultural context matters, religious context

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:06.900
matters, but it’s not everything.

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:11.930
So, there is clearly something accessible
here for audiences: especially when you break

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:14.900
it down to “cool dude fights monsters”.

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.200
I mean that’s just anime.

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:19.260
Which means-don’t be scared of BEOWULF.

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:21.920
Yeah, it’s old.

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:26.880
But it’s precisely because of its oldness
that it’s so incredibly fascinating.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:32.980
It’s been studied to death, and yet its
origins still remain so mysterious.

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:37.529
And because it’s so old, its influence over
the stories we read, watch, and tell today

00:12:37.529 --> 00:12:43.029
is so deep-rooted, that we’ve all already
experienced BEOWULF and its legacy whether

00:12:43.029 --> 00:12:55.760
we know it or not.

